  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several seawater electrolysis cell designs and highlights how they differ in ion transport, operating conditions, and performance. The top schematics show five approaches—alkaline water electrolysis, PEM, anion-exchange membrane, solid oxide, and a bipolar membrane system. In each, red arrows indicate circulation and ion movement between cathodic and anodic regions, emphasizing how membranes or layered electrolytes separate products while allowing charge transport. The drawings suggest different dominant ions and pH environments near each electrode.\n\nA central table summarizes representative catalysts, conditions, current density, durability, and electricity use. Most systems are shown operating around 1,000 mA cm⁻², but with noticeably different voltages, lifetimes, and energy consumption. The solid oxide route appears to have the lowest electricity demand, while the bipolar membrane example appears less durable and more energy intensive. A color scale indicates technical readiness from low to high.\n\nThe bottom illustrations focus on practical seawater management. One setup uses a water-binding membrane with concentrated KOH on both sides to feed water vapor from natural seawater while blocking problematic ions such as chloride, magnesium, calcium, and sulfate. Another shows natural seawater circulating at the cathode and NaOH at the anode, again aiming to control ion crossover and corrosion.",
